WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:07.134
Welcome to an Introduction to Retrieval Augmented&nbsp;
Generation or RAG for short.

00:00:07.134 --> 00:00:14.739
Before we dive into&nbsp;what RAG is, we need to first discuss the&nbsp;
common shortcomings of generative language&nbsp;models.

00:00:14.739 --> 00:00:23.757
Generative language models have picked&nbsp;up quite a bit of traction and use cases since&nbsp;2002 with the advent of ChatGPT.

00:00:23.757 --> 00:00:30.183
Their ability to&nbsp;answer questions and complete tasks with a simple&nbsp;prompt
makes them incredibly useful.

00:00:30.183 --> 00:00:37.218
However, these&nbsp;digital helpers are not without limitations.
One common issue is hallucinations.

00:00:37.218 --> 00:00:44.161
Imagine asking, "When&nbsp;were the Pyramids of Giza moved across the Golden&nbsp;Gate Bridge for the second time?

00:00:44.161 --> 00:00:52.140
And the language&nbsp;model confidently answering, "the Pyramids of Giza&nbsp;were moved for the second time in 1856."

00:00:52.140 --> 00:01:00.572
While&nbsp;obviously wrong, this behavior makes sense as&nbsp;LLMs original purpose was to predict the next&nbsp;token in a sequence,

00:01:00.572 --> 00:01:03.927
not factually answer user&nbsp;questions.

00:01:03.927 --> 00:01:07.771
Making matters worse, chatbots don't&nbsp;attribute their knowledge.

00:01:07.771 --> 00:01:17.830
By default, ChatGPT&nbsp;does not cite any external knowledge sources when&nbsp;it makes factual claims, we'd have to verify the&nbsp;information, ourselves.

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:21.301
Additionally, knowledge cut-offs can leave them outdated.

00:01:21.301 --> 00:01:33.000
For instance, asking&nbsp;a chatbot to write Next dot js code might result in&nbsp;the syntax of an older version of the framework, despite significant changes in recent releases.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:40.189
For example, GPT 3.5 has a knowledge cut-off of&nbsp;January, 2002.

00:01:40.189 --> 00:01:48.000
Another limitation is Context&nbsp;Window Size. If we want a model to 
answer&nbsp;questions based on a lengthy document,

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.061
pasting&nbsp;the entire document into the prompt might not&nbsp;work.

00:01:52.061 --> 00:01:58.480
Cutting-edge models are improving in this&nbsp;
area but larger prompts translate to higher costs.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:05.574
On the other hand, smaller models, while more&nbsp;
affordable, remain limited by their context size.

00:02:05.574 --> 00:02:14.361
Fortunately, a technique called Retrieval&nbsp;Augmented Generation or RAG, offers solutions&nbsp;to these problems.

00:02:14.361 --> 00:02:19.338
RAG essentially grants LLMs&nbsp;access to external knowledge sources.

00:02:19.338 --> 00:02:27.935
This can&nbsp;reduce hallucinations, enable the consultation of&nbsp;
your own sources, and keep the LLM's knowledge up to&nbsp;date.

00:02:27.935 --> 00:02:38.505
The external sources which RAG retrieves to&nbsp;augment the model's generations can be documents, databases, or even a live search of the internet.

00:02:38.505 --> 00:02:46.076
But for this course, we'll focus on documents,&nbsp;which are among the simplest and most common use&nbsp;cases.

00:02:46.076 --> 00:02:50.653
And RAG is more than a method of shoring up&nbsp;the weak points of LLMs.

00:02:50.653 --> 00:02:54.618
It also represents a new&nbsp;way to think about their functionality.

00:02:54.618 --> 00:03:02.796
Instead of&nbsp;storing information about the world inside the LLM's&nbsp;
weights, RAG makes it possible to store knowledge&nbsp;outside of the model,

00:03:02.796 --> 00:03:07.356
freeing up the model itself&nbsp;to focus on reasoning and cognition,

00:03:07.356 --> 00:03:13.249
while the&nbsp;retrieved knowledge source functions more like&nbsp;
a database in traditional software.

00:03:13.249 --> 00:03:23.224
At its core,&nbsp;RAG relies on embeddings which are rich numerical&nbsp;
representations of meaning derived usually from&nbsp;text chunks.

00:03:23.224 --> 00:03:28.968
However, they can also be made of other&nbsp;modalities,
such as image or audio.

00:03:28.968 --> 00:03:33.846
These embeddings&nbsp;capture the relationships
between different parts&nbsp;of the text.

00:03:33.846 --> 00:03:44.212
When the user asks a question, the&nbsp;question is converted into an embedding and is&nbsp;compared to the document embeddings, retrieving&nbsp;the most relevant information.

00:03:44.212 --> 00:03:48.000
This is commonly&nbsp;referred to as semantic search.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:56.933
These results are&nbsp;then injected into a prompt to the language model, which can reason about them, summarize them, and&nbsp;share them with the user.

00:03:56.933 --> 00:04:02.671
RAGs applications are&nbsp;vast. It can handle nonpublic information, like&nbsp;business documents,

00:04:02.671 --> 00:04:11.915
create a long-term memory&nbsp;for chatbots by embedding past conversations, and facilitate verifiable research by providing&nbsp;source attribution.

00:04:11.915 --> 00:04:19.239
Additionally, RAG can unearth&nbsp;specific information not
well-represented in the LLM's training data.

00:04:19.239 --> 00:04:24.124
Now that we've covered what RAG is
and where it fits in the LLM landscape,

00:04:24.124 --> 00:04:29.331
the rest&nbsp;of this course will dive further into what
we've&nbsp;discussed previously and more.

00:04:29.331 --> 00:04:38.440
According to our&nbsp;agenda, the next lesson should be Introduction to&nbsp;Embeddings and Similarity Search for approximately&nbsp;5 minutes,

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:42.231
followed up with a Search with Chroma&nbsp;exercise.

00:04:42.231 --> 00:04:50.366
Then we'll go into Document Chunking&nbsp;with RAG for approximately 7 minutes, along with&nbsp;an associated exercise.

00:04:50.366 --> 00:04:57.822
Then, we'll dive into Prompt&nbsp;Engineering for RAG for about 6 minutes,
followed&nbsp;up with its associated exercise.

00:04:57.822 --> 00:05:02.760
Then, we'll&nbsp;have a review exercise, and finally a quiz.

